why_this_one:
It is the whole of the client's Power Wave. ZzzCharacter.cpp:5045, in the damage arm of MoveCharacter - the same switch that makes the fireball and the bolt, keyed on the skill the packet carried:

    case AT_SKILL_POWERWAVE:
        if (o->Type == MODEL_ICE_QUEEN)
        {
            Angle[2] += 10.f;  CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
            Angle[2] -= 20.f;  CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
            Angle[2] += 10.f;
        }
        CreateEffect(MODEL_MAGIC2, o->Position, Angle, o->Light);
        PlayBuffer(SOUND_MAGIC);

One wave for a player and for the Hell Spider; three in a ten-degree fan for the Ice Queen, which is the only monster in 0.75 that gets a shape of its own out of this arm. Angle is the local the same function set at line 4891 - `Angle[2] = CreateAngle2D(o->Position, to->Position)` - so it is aimed along the heading to the target and not along the caster's facing, exactly as Fire Ball is.

Two monsters carry the skill in 0.75: the Ice Queen on Devias (number 25, level 52) and the Hell Spider in Dungeon (number 13, level 40). So this model has to work thrown by an animal as well as by a wizard, the way the meteor already does for the Lich.

glow_from:
The mover's own, and it is the blue one:

    Vector(Luminosity * 0.3f, Luminosity * 0.6f, Luminosity, Light);
    AddTerrainLight(o->Position[0], o->Position[1], Light, 3, PrimaryTerrainLight);

Range three, where the bolt and the meteor both light two tiles. Luminosity is MoveEffect's shared roll - 0.7 to 1.0, going out over the last five frames - so this is the same flicker every effect in the game carries, tinted blue and thrown a tile wider.

smoke_from:
Four a frame, each on its own coin:

    for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++)
        if (rand_fps_check(1))
            CreateParticleFpsChecked(BITMAP_SMOKE, o->Position, o->Angle, o->Light, 3);

Eighty over the flight at MU's clock, with a ten-frame life, so around forty are alive at once. That is by a distance the densest trail of the three spells built so far - the bolt lays one particle a frame and the fireball one every fifty units - and it is the number to measure before anything else.

BITMAP_SMOKE sub-type 3, whose own rules are in ZzzEffectParticle.cpp and belong to the particle rather than to this spell: born with LifeTime 10 and a scale of 0.80 to 1.11, a pitch of `rand() % 90 - 45` and a roll of `rand() % 360`, and a velocity of 40 to 47 units a frame along that rotated axis - so it sprays in a cone forty-five degrees either side. Its mover tints it faintly blue (`0.8L, 0.8L, L` off `LifeTime / 8`), grows the scale a tenth a frame so it roughly doubles, and multiplies the velocity by 0.4 *every frame*. That last is not scaled by FPS_ANIMATION_FACTOR in the original and has to become a power of the factor here or the drag changes with the machine. The compounding is severe: a puff travels about 67 units in total, two thirds of a tile, and stops.

scroll_from:
**The one piece of machinery this spell needs that nothing in MU2 has yet.**

    o->BlendMeshTexCoordU = -(float)o->LifeTime * 0.2f;

The U coordinate of the blend mesh is set every frame off the remaining lifetime, so it runs from -4.0 at birth to 0 as the wave dies: four full repeats of the sheet streaming along the model over four fifths of a second. The sheet is 128 wide and the model's UVs span exactly 0 to 1, so this needs the texture wrapped rather than clamped, and it is what makes the thing read as a wave moving through itself rather than as a shape being carried.
